From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Burke Cates <burke.cates@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 18:56:23 -0800
Subject: [PATCH 25/27] NOT-FOR-UPSTREAM: ASoC: SDCA: power an output's PDE down before the path
 that feeds it

A Power Domain Entity is registered as a DAPM supply, and DAPM powers
supplies down last. For a PDE that powers an output (the headphone
amplifier behind an Output Terminal) that means the host side feeding
the Function - the SoundWire data port and the source that drives it -
is torn down while the amplifier is still on, and whatever the teardown
puts on the wire is played.

On the ASUS Zenbook A16 (Glymur, WCD9378 SimpleJack on SoundWire, fed
by the LPASS RX macro) every playback stop gave one click on the
headphones, -7.6 to -12.3 dBFS peak at the jack with the codec at 0 dB
(single-sample impulse plus a small DC step, 10/10 stops). The DAPM
trace shows the order: RX macro interpolators POST_PMD, RX_MCLK off,
then PDE 47 (HPH) to PS3 over the next 30 ms, then the SoundWire stream
is disabled.

Give non-dataport Output Terminals a PRE_PMD event that takes each PDE
feeding them to PS3 first, if DAPM is about to power that PDE down
anyway (power set, new_power clear), so a PDE shared with a terminal
that stays on is left alone. The PDE's own power-down then finds PS3
already and its poll returns at once. Output terminal widgets are
speaker type and power down before muxes, mixers and supplies, so this
runs before the host path stops.

With this the A16 gave 0 clicks above -55 dBFS in 15 stops at 0 dB
(noise peaks of the loopback rig are about -57 dBFS; a module-parameter
variant of the same code gave 0 in 20 more), and playback, the speakers
and the PDE power-up were unaffected (bead rnf).

Assisted-by: Claude Code (Opus 5.5)
---
Not-for-upstream: local workaround for one laptop, not reviewed for
  submission; use only for local testing.

 sound/soc/sdca/sdca_asoc.c | 55 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 55 insertions(+)

diff --git a/sound/soc/sdca/sdca_asoc.c b/sound/soc/sdca/sdca_asoc.c
index 559a0fdfd98e..565ce96af6fb 100644
--- a/sound/soc/sdca/sdca_asoc.c
+++ b/sound/soc/sdca/sdca_asoc.c
@@ -330,6 +330,59 @@ static int entity_parse_it(struct device *dev,
 	return 0;
 }
 
+static int entity_pde_event(struct snd_soc_dapm_widget *widget,
+			    struct snd_kcontrol *kctl, int event);
+
+/*
+ * A PDE is a DAPM supply, so DAPM powers it down last, after the rest of
+ * the path. When a PDE powers an output (an amplifier behind an Output
+ * Terminal), the host side that feeds the Function, a SoundWire data
+ * port and whatever drives it, has then already been torn down while the
+ * amplifier is still on, and anything that teardown puts on the wire is
+ * heard. Take such a PDE to PS3 when its terminal starts to power down,
+ * if DAPM is about to power the PDE down anyway; the PDE's own power-down
+ * then finds it in PS3 already.
+ */
+static int entity_ot_pde_event(struct snd_soc_dapm_widget *widget,
+			       struct snd_kcontrol *kctl, int event)
+{
+	struct snd_soc_component *component = snd_soc_dapm_to_component(widget->dapm);
+	struct snd_soc_dapm_path *path;
+
+	if (!component || event != SND_SOC_DAPM_PRE_PMD)
+		return 0;
+
+	snd_soc_dapm_widget_for_each_source_path(widget, path) {
+		struct snd_soc_dapm_widget *pde = path->source;
+		struct sdca_entity *entity = pde->priv;
+		unsigned int val;
+		int ret;
+
+		if (pde->event != entity_pde_event || !pde->power || pde->new_power)
+			continue;
+
+		ret = regmap_read(component->regmap, pde->reg, &val);
+		if (ret || val == pde->off_val)
+			continue;
+
+		ret = regmap_write(component->regmap, pde->reg, pde->off_val);
+		if (ret)
+			continue;
+
+		ret = sdca_asoc_pde_poll_actual_ps(component->regmap,
+						   SDW_SDCA_CTL_FUNC(pde->reg),
+						   SDW_SDCA_CTL_ENT(pde->reg),
+						   pde->on_val, pde->off_val,
+						   entity->pde.max_delay,
+						   entity->pde.num_max_delay);
+		if (ret)
+			dev_warn(component->dev, "%s: early power down of %s failed: %d\n",
+				 widget->name, entity->label, ret);
+	}
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
 static int entity_parse_ot(struct device *dev,
 			   struct sdca_function_data *function,
 			   struct sdca_entity *entity,
@@ -349,6 +402,8 @@ static int entity_parse_ot(struct device *dev,
 		add_route(route, aif_name, NULL, entity->label);
 	} else {
 		(*widget)->id = snd_soc_dapm_spk;
+		(*widget)->event_flags = SND_SOC_DAPM_PRE_PMD;
+		(*widget)->event = entity_ot_pde_event;
 	}
 
 	if (entity->iot.clock)
